@attribute [Route(pageUrl)]
@layout DemosMainLayout

<PageMetaTags PageUrl="@pageUrl" Title="@metaTitle" Description="@metaDescription" ImageUrl="@imageUrl" />

<PageHero Title="@pageTitle">
    <SubTitleTemplate>
        @((MarkupString)pageDescription)
    </SubTitleTemplate>
</PageHero>

<DocsLink Href="@DemoRouteConstants.Docs_BubbleChart" />

<Prerequisites PageUrl="@pageUrl" />

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="How it works" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="how-it-works">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Bubble Chart</strong> component visualizes three dimensions of data by displaying each data point as a bubble, where the X and Y coordinates determine the position and the bubble size represents a third value. This makes it ideal for comparing and analyzing relationships between multiple variables.
        <br />
        <br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add the <code>BubbleChart</code> component to your page and set its desired <code>Width</code> (e.g., <code>Width="600"</code>).</li>
                <li>Prepare your <code>ChartData</code> with <code>Labels</code> for the X-axis and one or more <code>BubbleChartDataset</code> objects, each containing a list of <code>BubbleChartDataPoint</code> (with <code>X</code>, <code>Y</code>, and <code>R</code> values).</li>
                <li>Configure chart appearance and behavior using <code>BubbleChartOptions</code> (e.g., responsiveness, tooltips, axis settings).</li>
                <li>Initialize the chart by calling <code>InitializeAsync</code> with your data and options.</li>
                <li>Use built-in methods such as <code>AddDatasetAsync</code>, <code>AddDataAsync</code>, and <code>UpdateAsync</code> to dynamically update the chart as shown in the demo.</li>
                <li>Refer to the demo code below for practical examples, including adding datasets, randomizing data, and customizing chart styles.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        This demo provides a hands-on example of setting up a Bubble Chart in Blazor, demonstrating how to bind and update your data for interactive visualizations.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BubbleChart_Demo_01_Examples)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Click event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="click-event">
    <Block>
        Set the <code>OnClick</code> callback to react when a bubble is selected. The callback receives <code>ChartClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset, point index, and raw value; the demo displays the selected item below the chart.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BubbleChart_Demo_02_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Export image" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="export-image">
    <Block>
        Export the chart as an image with <code>ToBase64ImageAsync</code>, which returns a base64 data URL you can show, store or send, or save it straight to a file in the browser with <code>DownloadImageAsync</code>.
        If the chart is still animating, both wait for the animation to finish, and the image is streamed from the browser, so Blazor Server needs no larger message size.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Keep a reference to the chart with <code>@@ref</code> and initialize it.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ToBase64ImageAsync()</code> for a PNG data URL, or pass <code>ChartImageType.Jpeg</code> and a quality from 0 to 1.</li>
                <li>Call <code>DownloadImageAsync("bubble-chart.png")</code> to save the image as a file without sending it to .NET.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BubbleChart_Demo_03_Export_Image)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Show and hide datasets" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="show-and-hide-datasets">
    <Block>
        Show and hide whole datasets or single bubbles from C#, check whether a dataset is visible and count the visible datasets.
        Visibility changed at runtime, from C# or a legend click, stays in place when <code>UpdateAsync</code> refreshes the data, because datasets and bubbles are matched by label.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>HideAsync(datasetIndex)</code> and <code>ShowAsync(datasetIndex)</code>, or <code>SetDatasetVisibilityAsync(datasetIndex, visible)</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass a data index too, for example <code>HideAsync(0, 2)</code>, to hide one bubble.</li>
                <li>Read the state with <code>IsDatasetVisibleAsync</code> and <code>GetVisibleDatasetCountAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Refresh with <code>UpdateAsync(data, options, ChartUpdateMode.None)</code> to apply new data without animation; hidden datasets and bubbles stay hidden.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BubbleChart_Demo_04_Show_Hide_Datasets)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Legend click event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="legend-click-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnLegendClick</code> to react when a legend item is clicked. The callback receives <code>ChartLegendClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset index, the legend text and whether the dataset is hidden after the click.
        Set <code>LegendClickToggles="false"</code> to stop the legend from hiding datasets, for example to open details instead.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnLegendClick</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Keep <code>LegendClickToggles</code> at <code>true</code> for the default hide and show, or set it to <code>false</code> to handle the click yourself.</li>
                <li>Use <code>DatasetIndex</code>, <code>Text</code> and <code>Hidden</code> from the event arguments.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BubbleChart_Demo_05_Legend_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Hover event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="hover-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnHover</code> to react when the pointer moves onto a different bubble. The callback receives <code>ChartHoverEventArgs</code> with the same item details as <code>OnClick</code>.
        It is called only when the hovered bubble changes, and once with a <code>null</code> <code>DatasetIndex</code> when the pointer leaves all bubbles, so it does not flood Blazor Server with calls.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnHover</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Check <code>DatasetIndex</code>: a value means a bubble is hovered; <code>null</code> means the pointer left all bubbles.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BubbleChart_Demo_06_Hover_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Programmatic highlight" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="programmatic-highlight">
    <Block>
        Highlight bubbles and show their tooltip from C# with <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>, for example to point at a value from a table next to the chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Pass one or more <code>ChartActiveElement(datasetIndex, index)</code> items to <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass an empty list to clear the highlight and the tooltip.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BubbleChart_Demo_07_Programmatic_Highlight)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Update modes and animation control" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="update-modes-and-animation-control">
    <Block>
        Choose how <code>UpdateAsync</code> animates with <code>ChartUpdateMode</code>, and control the running animation with <code>StopAsync</code>, <code>RenderAsync</code>, <code>ResetAsync</code> and <code>ClearAsync</code>.
        These map one-to-one to the Chart.js <code>update(mode)</code>, <code>stop()</code>, <code>render()</code>, <code>reset()</code> and <code>clear()</code> methods.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> for the default animated update, or pass <code>ChartUpdateMode.None</code> to apply the change at once. Each update replaces the chart's datasets, so an animated update draws the new data from the chart's starting state.</li>
                <li>Call <code>StopAsync()</code> to end any running animation; the chart stays as it was drawn at that moment. Call <code>UpdateAsync</code> again to animate to new values.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> to put the chart back in its starting state, before its first animation, and <code>RenderAsync()</code> to draw that state. Stop any running animation first, or its next frame draws over the reset.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ClearAsync()</code> to clear the canvas; <code>RenderAsync()</code> draws the chart again. A running animation draws over the cleared canvas on its next frame, so stop it first or wait for it to end.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(BubbleChart_Demo_08_Update_Modes)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

@code {
    private const string pageUrl = DemoRouteConstants.Demos_BubbleChart;
    private const string pageTitle = "Bubble Chart";
    private const string pageDescription = "The <code>Blazor Bubble Chart</code> component is used to display three dimensions of data at the same time. The location of the bubble is determined by the first two dimensions and the corresponding horizontal and vertical axes. The third dimension is represented by the size of the individual bubbles.";
    private const string metaTitle = "Blazor Bubble Chart";
    private const string metaDescription = "The Blazor Bubble Chart component is used to display three dimensions of data at the same time. The location of the bubble is determined by the first two dimensions and the corresponding horizontal and vertical axes. The third dimension is represented by the size of the individual bubbles.";
    private const string imageUrl = DemoImageSrcConstants.BubbleChart;
}
